WORK SUMMARY
================================================================================

Developed the tidal field and stripping analysis extension module for the
Milky Way–Andromeda merger simulation pipeline. This section expands the
previous density and angular momentum analysis framework by introducing
mechanical binding calculations, tidal disruption measurements, energetic
heating diagnostics, and structural deformation tracking.

The initial software scaffold was generated using Claude as a structural
starting point. I then modified, expanded, and integrated the
framework with my own analysis methodology, physical diagnostics, numerical
implementations, and visualization pipeline.

The final module was designed as a physically motivated extension of the
existing density_pipeline.py and section21_angular_momentum.py frameworks,
using shared simulation states and orbital parameters.

================================================================================
SCIENTIFIC OBJECTIVES
================================================================================

The primary objective of this section was to quantify how tidal interactions
during the MW–M31 encounter reshape galaxy structure through:

1. Jacobi Radius Evolution:
   - Implemented Roche/Jacobi tidal boundary calculations to measure the
     progressive erosion of gravitationally stable regions.
   - Tracked the evolution of the tidal radius as the progenitors approach
     and interact.
   - Compared tidal limits against characteristic core scales.

2. Mechanical Binding & Unbinding Analysis:
   - Developed an energetic particle classification framework using:

          E_total = E_kin + E_pot

     where particles satisfying:

          E_total < 0

     are classified as gravitationally bound.

   - Generated time-dependent measurements of:
        • Bound mass fraction
        • Escaped/unbound debris mass
        • Radial density distribution of retained and stripped material

3. Tidal Heating Diagnostics:
   - Implemented kinetic energy evolution tracking to identify impulsive
     heating events.
   - Calculated:

          ΔE_kin(r,t)

     profiles to identify regions undergoing energy injection.
   - Used temporal gradients to study shell inflation and relaxation.

4. Tidal Tensor Morphology:
   - Constructed numerical tidal tensor estimates through finite-difference
     potential derivatives.
   - Diagonalized the tidal tensor matrix to extract principal deformation
     modes.
   - Developed anisotropy measurements describing compression versus
     stretching regimes.

5. Structural Shape Evolution:
   - Implemented reduced inertia tensor calculations.
   - Extracted axis ratios:

          b/a
          c/a

     to quantify morphological transformation.
   - Evaluated shape evolution separately across:
        • Inner core
        • Intermediate halo
        • Outer tidal regions

